<Article slug="stoneware">
  <p>
    &ldquo;Stoneware, fired to cone 10.&rdquo; It is a short phrase on every Milzy piece, and it
    says a great deal about how the work will live with you.
  </p>

  <h2>Three families of clay</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Earthenware</strong> is fired at lower temperatures. It is often warm in colour and
      easy to work, but it stays slightly porous and is more prone to chipping.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stoneware</strong> is fired hot enough for the clay to <em>vitrify</em>: its
      particles partly melt and fuse, so the body becomes dense, strong and resistant to water.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Porcelain</strong> is a refined, white, very high-fired clay, prized for its
      translucency.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a cone is</h2>
  <p>
    Potters measure firings with <em>pyrometric cones</em>: small slender pyramids of ceramic
    material designed to soften and bend once they have absorbed a certain amount of heat. They
    measure heat work, temperature over time, which is what actually matures a glaze and a clay.
    Cone 10 is at the high end of the scale, at roughly 2,350&deg;F (1,285&deg;C).
  </p>

  <h2>Why it matters to you</h2>
  <p>
    A pot fired to cone 10 is a pot built for use. The dense stoneware body is strong and holds
    heat well, which is part of why a good stoneware mug keeps coffee warm. The surface the soda
    creates is fused to the clay rather than sitting on top of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is also why handmade stoneware rewards a little care. See the
    <a href="/care/">care notes</a> for how to keep a piece at its best for decades.
  </p>
</Article>
